1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,301
About sixty-five million American homes have a thermostat that can adjust itself automatically.

2
00:00:06,601 --> 00:00:08,942
Only about twenty million actually let it.

3
00:00:09,242 --> 00:00:11,757
So does a smart thermostat really save you money?

4
00:00:12,057 --> 00:00:16,076
Or is it just an expensive way to change the temperature from your phone?

5
00:00:16,376 --> 00:00:30,444
In the next few minutes, I'll answer that with real numbers: what these thermostats are actually required to prove, what that's worth in dollars for a typical home, and the one habit that decides whether yours pays off.

6
00:00:30,744 --> 00:00:31,662
No guesses.

7
00:00:31,962 --> 00:00:39,224
Everything here comes from ENERGY STAR, the Department of Energy, and the government's own household energy survey.

8
00:00:39,524 --> 00:00:43,046
First question: how much money are we even talking about?

9
00:00:43,346 --> 00:00:50,853
According to ENERGY STAR, the average American household spends more than nine hundred dollars a year on heating and cooling.

10
00:00:51,153 --> 00:00:54,091
That's almost half of a typical home energy bill.

11
00:00:54,391 --> 00:01:00,231
Using the government's household survey and today's prices, I get numbers in the same range.

12
00:01:00,531 --> 00:01:06,663
A home heated with natural gas spends about six hundred sixty-five dollars a year on heating.

13
00:01:06,963 --> 00:01:14,467
A home with central air spends about five hundred five dollars a year on cooling, including the fan that moves the air.

14
00:01:14,767 --> 00:01:18,868
So the thermostat controls the biggest single chunk of your energy bill.

15
00:01:19,168 --> 00:01:21,790
That's why even a small percentage matters here.

16
00:01:22,090 --> 00:01:26,254
Second question: what does a smart thermostat actually do differently?

17
00:01:26,554 --> 00:01:29,680
A basic thermostat holds whatever temperature you set.

18
00:01:29,980 --> 00:01:34,035
About forty-four million American homes still have one of those.

19
00:01:34,335 --> 00:01:37,664
A programmable one follows a schedule that you enter.

20
00:01:37,964 --> 00:01:40,110
A smart thermostat goes a step further.

21
00:01:40,410 --> 00:01:48,710
ENERGY STAR describes features like geofencing, which uses your phone to notice when you've left for the day and set your system back.

22
00:01:49,010 --> 00:01:57,658
And learning, where certain models figure out your preferences on their own and adjust to energy-saving temperatures when you're asleep or away.

23
00:01:57,958 --> 00:02:04,030
The idea behind all of it is simple: your heating and cooling should run less when nobody needs them.

24
00:02:04,330 --> 00:02:08,404
Third question, and it's the big one: does it actually save energy?

25
00:02:08,704 --> 00:02:10,645
Here's the part most reviews skip.

26
00:02:10,945 --> 00:02:17,063
To earn the ENERGY STAR label, a smart thermostat has to prove its savings with real-world data.

27
00:02:17,363 --> 00:02:25,316
The companies analyze a full year of data from hundreds of their customers' homes, using software provided by the EPA.

28
00:02:25,616 --> 00:02:31,475
Run time simply means how many hours your furnace or air conditioner is actually running.

29
00:02:31,775 --> 00:02:33,489
Less run time, less energy.

30
00:02:33,789 --> 00:02:35,208
And the bar is specific.

31
00:02:35,508 --> 00:02:41,962
Across the national average, certified thermostats have to cut heating run time by at least eight percent,

32
00:02:42,262 --> 00:02:44,720
and cooling run time by at least ten percent.

33
00:02:45,020 --> 00:02:47,403
There's also a floor for the weaker results.

34
00:02:47,703 --> 00:02:55,310
Four out of five homes in the sample have to save at least four percent on heating, and at least five percent on cooling.

35
00:02:55,610 --> 00:03:00,404
So a certified smart thermostat isn't just a promise printed on a box.

36
00:03:00,704 --> 00:03:03,091
Its savings are measured in real homes.

37
00:03:03,391 --> 00:03:06,750
Fourth question: what is that worth in actual dollars?

38
00:03:07,050 --> 00:03:12,759
I'll assume that cutting how long your system runs cuts its energy use by about the same amount.

39
00:03:13,059 --> 00:03:17,445
That's a simplification, but a reasonable one for most standard systems.

40
00:03:17,745 --> 00:03:20,133
Take a home with gas heat and central air.

41
00:03:20,433 --> 00:03:26,031
Eight percent of six hundred sixty-five dollars in heating is about fifty-three dollars.

42
00:03:26,331 --> 00:03:30,777
Ten percent of five hundred five dollars in cooling is about fifty.

43
00:03:31,077 --> 00:03:36,878
Together, that's roughly a hundred and four dollars a year, at the certified national average.

44
00:03:37,178 --> 00:03:42,719
For four out of five homes, savings come out to at least about fifty-two dollars a year.

45
00:03:43,019 --> 00:03:49,296
An all-electric home, with electric heat and central air, lands at around ninety dollars a year.

46
00:03:49,596 --> 00:03:58,569
And if you start from ENERGY STAR's nine hundred dollar figure instead, eight to ten percent works out to roughly seventy-two to ninety dollars.

47
00:03:58,869 --> 00:04:05,247
So a realistic range for a typical home is somewhere between fifty and a hundred dollars a year.

48
00:04:05,547 --> 00:04:08,099
Before we go on, does it matter where you live?

49
00:04:08,399 --> 00:04:10,807
It does, but maybe not the way you'd expect.

50
00:04:11,107 --> 00:04:19,785
In the Midwest, a gas-heated home spends about nine hundred thirty dollars a year on heating, and about three hundred thirty on cooling.

51
00:04:20,085 --> 00:04:27,283
At the certified minimum, that's around a hundred and eight dollars a year in savings, and most of it comes from heating.

52
00:04:27,583 --> 00:04:28,884
In the South, it flips.

53
00:04:29,184 --> 00:04:34,417
Heating is only about five hundred dollars, but cooling is around seven hundred eight.

54
00:04:34,717 --> 00:04:40,116
Savings come out to about a hundred and eleven, and most of that comes from the air conditioner.

55
00:04:40,416 --> 00:04:47,054
The Northeast lands near ninety-three dollars, and the West, with milder weather on both ends, near eighty.

56
00:04:47,354 --> 00:04:50,638
So the total is surprisingly similar across the country.

57
00:04:50,938 --> 00:04:53,879
What changes is which season does the saving.

58
00:04:54,179 --> 00:04:58,382
In a cold state, the setback that matters is the one on winter nights.

59
00:04:58,682 --> 00:05:03,967
In a hot state, it's the one on summer afternoons, while everyone is out of the house.

60
00:05:04,267 --> 00:05:08,571
Fifth question: if the savings are real, why don't more people get them?

61
00:05:08,871 --> 00:05:11,927
This is the most revealing number in the whole survey.

62
00:05:12,227 --> 00:05:23,413
According to the government's Residential Energy Consumption Survey, about fifty-two and a half million homes have a programmable thermostat, and almost thirteen million have a smart one.

63
00:05:23,713 --> 00:05:29,417
Smart ones are still fairly rare: only about twelve percent of the homes that have a thermostat.

64
00:05:29,717 --> 00:05:34,786
That's around sixty-five million homes that could adjust the temperature automatically.

65
00:05:35,086 --> 00:05:39,222
But only about twenty million homes say their thermostat actually does it.

66
00:05:39,522 --> 00:05:41,022
That's about three in ten.

67
00:05:41,322 --> 00:05:47,252
Meanwhile, about fifty-one million homes set one temperature and leave it there most of the time.

68
00:05:47,552 --> 00:05:50,449
Another thirty-one million adjust it by hand.

69
00:05:50,749 --> 00:05:55,827
In other words, millions of people already own the tool, and simply aren't using it.

70
00:05:56,127 --> 00:06:00,982
And that matters, because a smart thermostat doesn't save energy by being smart.

71
00:06:01,282 --> 00:06:04,816
It saves energy by lowering the temperature when you don't need it.

72
00:06:05,116 --> 00:06:09,152
Sixth question: do you even need a smart thermostat to save money?

73
00:06:09,452 --> 00:06:10,663
Not necessarily.

74
00:06:10,963 --> 00:06:20,500
The Department of Energy says that setting your thermostat back for at least eight hours a day can save you as much as ten percent a year on heating and cooling.

75
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:27,305
For that home with gas heat and central air, ten percent is up to about a hundred seventeen dollars a year.

76
00:06:27,605 --> 00:06:30,871
Any thermostat can do that, if you're disciplined about it.

77
00:06:31,171 --> 00:06:35,903
You just have to remember to do it every single night, and every time you leave.

78
00:06:36,203 --> 00:06:38,345
That's where the smart one earns its keep.

79
00:06:38,645 --> 00:06:44,471
Its real value is that it does the setback for you, every day, without you having to think about it.

80
00:06:44,771 --> 00:06:50,925
And you'll sometimes hear that turning the heat down makes your system work harder later, so you don't really save.

81
00:06:51,225 --> 00:06:56,748
The Department of Energy's own advice points the other way: set it back, and you save.

82
00:06:57,048 --> 00:07:00,211
Last question: will one pay for itself in your home?

83
00:07:00,511 --> 00:07:01,676
Here's the quick math.

84
00:07:01,976 --> 00:07:07,116
Take the price you'd pay for the thermostat, and divide it by what you'd save each year.

85
00:07:07,416 --> 00:07:16,687
If a thermostat costs, say, a hundred fifty dollars, and you save about a hundred dollars a year, it pays for itself in about a year and a half.

86
00:07:16,987 --> 00:07:19,714
If you only save fifty, it takes three years.

87
00:07:20,014 --> 00:07:30,916
You'll save more if your home sits empty for long stretches, like during the workday, if your heating and cooling bills are above average, and if you actually let the schedule run.

88
00:07:31,216 --> 00:07:37,797
You'll save less if someone is home all day, or if you already turn the temperature down by hand every night.

89
00:07:38,097 --> 00:07:40,373
So, does a smart thermostat save money?

90
00:07:40,673 --> 00:07:41,244
Yes.

91
00:07:41,544 --> 00:07:51,246
Certified models have to prove at least eight to ten percent less run time, which works out to roughly fifty to a hundred dollars a year for a typical home.

92
00:07:51,546 --> 00:07:53,707
But the bigger lesson is in that survey.

93
00:07:54,007 --> 00:07:56,182
The savings don't come from the device.

94
00:07:56,482 --> 00:07:58,954
They come from actually setting the temperature back.

95
00:07:59,254 --> 00:08:02,579
About sixty-five million homes could do it automatically.

96
00:08:02,879 --> 00:08:04,522
Only around twenty million do.

97
00:08:04,822 --> 00:08:10,102
So if you already have a programmable thermostat, check that its schedule is actually on.

98
00:08:10,402 --> 00:08:16,637
If you're buying a new one, look for the ENERGY STAR label, because that's the one with measured savings.

99
00:08:16,937 --> 00:08:21,881
And whichever you have, the rule is the same: lower it when you're asleep or away.

100
00:08:22,181 --> 00:08:29,330
If you want to know what else in your home costs the most to run, my full ranking of fifteen appliances is on this channel.
